</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
To calculate the entropy change (\(\Delta S\)) for the reaction, we use the formula:

\[
\Delta S = \sum S_{\text{products}} - \sum S_{\text{reactants}}
\]

**Step 1: Calculate the total entropy of the products:**
\[
2 \text{PbO(s)}: 2 \times 66.5 = 133.0 \text{ J/K}
\]
\[
2 \text{SO}_2(g): 2 \times 248.2 = 496.4 \text{ J/K}
\]
\[
\text{Total entropy of products} = 133.0 + 496.4 = 629.4 \text{ J/K}
\]

**Step 2: Calculate the total entropy of the reactants:**
\[
2 \text{PbS(s)}: 2 \times 91.2 = 182.4 \text{ J/K}
\]
\[
3 \text{O}_2(g): 3 \times 205.1 = 615.3 \text{ J/K}
\]
\[
\text{Total entropy of reactants} = 182.4 + 615.3 = 797.7 \text{ J/K}
\]

**Step 3: Compute the entropy change:**
\[
\Delta S = 629.4 - 797.7 = -168.3 \text{ J/K}
\]

**Final Answer:**
The entropy change for the reaction is \(\boxed{-168.3 \text{ J/K}}\). 

**Note:** The original answer provided was \(-113.5\), which is incorrect based on the given data and calculations. The correct answer is \(-168.3\).
</GROUND_TRUTH>

