","Let $S_n = \sum_{n^2 + 1}^2$
We have $S_n = (x+1)^2 + (x^2+1)^2 + (x^3+1)^2 + \left(x^n+1\right)^2$.
We can write each term as
$(x+1)^2 = x^2 + 2x + 1$
$(x^2+1)^2 = x^4 + 2x^2 + 1$
$(x^3+1)^2 = x^6 + 2x^3 + 1$
$(x^n+1)^2 = x^{2n} + 2x^n + 1$
So
$$S_n = \sum_{_n^n} (x^n + 1)^2 = \sum_n (x^{2n} + 2x^n + 1)$$
$$ = \sum_n x^{2n} + 2 \sum_n x^n + \sum_n 1 $$
$$ = \sum_n (x^{2n} + 2x^n + 1) $$
We have
$$\sum_n x^{2n} = \sum_n (x^2)^n = \frac{(x^2)^{n+1}}{1 - x^2} = \frac{x^{2(n+1)}}{1 - x^2}$$
$$\sum_n x^n = \frac{x^{n+1}}{1 - x}$$
$$\sum_n 1 = n$$
So
$$S_n = \frac{x^{2(n+1)}{1 - x^2} + 2 \frac{x^{n+1}}{1 - x} + n$$
However, this is not a sum.
We are given $S_n = (x + 1)^2 + (x^2 + 1)^2 + \left(x^3 + 1\right)^2 + \left(x^n + 1\right)^2$.
We can write
$$S_n = \sum_{_n^n} (x^n + 1)^2 = \sum_n (x^{2n} + 2x^n + 1)$$
$$ = \sum_n x^{2n} + 2 \sum_n x^n + \sum_n 1 $$
$$ = \sum_{_n^n} x^{2n} + 2 \sum_n x^n + n $$
$$ = \sum_{_n^n} (x^2)^n + 2 \sum_n x^n + n $$
$$ = \frac{(x^2)^{n+1}}{1 - x^2} + \frac{2x^{n+1}}{1 - x} + n $$
$$ = \frac{x^{2n+2}}{1 - x^2} + \frac{2x^{n+1}}{1 - x} + n $$
If $x=2$, we have
$S_n = (2+1)^)^2 + (4+1)^2 + (8+1)^^2 + (8+1)^2 = 3^2 + , +
$$ = 9 + 25 + 9 + 1 +
$$ = 39 + 9 + 1 = 49 + 9 = 50 $$
