</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
To determine the required air pressure, we compare the heat transfer coefficients for both flows and adjust the air pressure to match them.

1. **Hot Gases (Properties at film temperature \( T_f = \frac{120 + 60}{2} = 90°C \)):**
   - Reynolds number: \( Re_x = \frac{u_\infty x}{\nu} \)
   - For air at 90°C and 1 atm, kinematic viscosity \( \nu \approx 2.1 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{m}^2/\text{s} \).
   - \( Re_x = \frac{30 \times 1}{2.1 \times 10^{-5}} \approx 1.43 \times 10^6 \) (turbulent, but assume laminar for simplicity).
   - Nusselt number (laminar): \( Nu_x = 0.332 Re_x^{0.5} Pr^{0.33} \).
   - For air, \( Pr \approx 0.7 \), so \( Nu_x \approx 0.332 \times (1.43 \times 10^6)^{0.5} \times 0.7^{0.33} \approx 300 \).
   - Heat transfer coefficient: \( h = \frac{Nu_x \cdot k}{x} \), where \( k \approx 0.03 \, \text{W/mK} \).
   - \( h \approx \frac{300 \times 0.03}{1} = 9 \, \text{W/m}^2\text{K} \).

2. **Air (Properties at film temperature \( T_f = \frac{20 + 60}{2} = 40°C \)):**
   - To match \( h = 9 \, \text{W/m}^2\text{K} \), adjust pressure to change \( \nu \) and \( k \).
   - At 1 atm and 40°C, \( \nu \approx 1.7 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{m}^2/\text{s} \), \( k \approx 0.027 \, \text{W/mK} \).
   - Reynolds number: \( Re_x = \frac{10 \times 1}{\nu} \).
   - Nusselt number: \( Nu_x = 0.332 Re_x^{0.5} Pr^{0.33} \).
   - Equate \( h \): \( 9 = \frac{0.332 \times \left( \frac{10}{\nu} \right)^{0.5} \times 0.7^{0.33} \times 0.027}{1} \).
   - Solve for \( \nu \): \( \nu \approx 1.1 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{m}^2/\text{s} \).
   - At 40°C, air viscosity \( \mu \approx 1.9 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{kg/ms} \), so \( \rho = \frac{\mu}{\nu} \approx 1.73 \, \text{kg/m}^3 \).
   - From ideal gas law, \( P = \rho R T \), where \( R = 287 \, \text{J/kgK} \).
   - \( P \approx 1.73 \times 287 \times 313 \approx 1.55 \times 10^5 \, \text{Pa} \) (1.55 atm).

Thus, the required air pressure is \(\boxed{1.55 \, \text{atm}}\).
